A||||3||Seanh||Anyone good at rebuilding auto tranny?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||02-03-2001||11:34 AM||||I wasnt going to rebuild my transmission when I do the 3.4 swap, but now I wonder if I should. How hard is it? Somthing I can do myself? (Or should I say, Somthing I SHOULD do myself   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) Do they sell some sorts for overhaul kits for it? BTW, Its a 4spd. Auto.<BR>The reason I ask is because I am starting to think I want to stick with the Auto as opposed to going to the 5spd like I was planning on.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">     <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto (3.4 Conversion in Progress)<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I><p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 02-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||02-03-2001||11:46 AM||||Aamco or some other transmission shop should take on a rebuild on an automatic transmission, not a job for the inexperienced to tackle.  Rather pricy also.  I'd expect $800-1300.||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||02-03-2001||12:22 PM||||Not something most people should try themselves. You could have one done. You could look into a GM new or rebuilt. Allot of the GM part prices for things like this are suprisingly competitive.<P>If you want to try this youself you can get the parts here. <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>Transmission Exchange Co.</A> They offer parts and full rebuilds. I think they may even have manuals. You would need a manual and All the TSB's from GM for the tranny before you can rebuild it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||02-03-2001||02:15 PM||||I rebuilt the AOD trans for my 86 F150 with much success. Everybody talks like it's impossible but it really wasn't too bad. If you have patience and know how to follow directions it's a piece of cake. I used a Haynes manual that takes you through it step by step. A local shop wanted $900 to rebuild it, it cost me like $150 for parts, took about 6 hours if I remember correctly. I was very nervous about doing it, but it worked out well. Only you can decide if you think you can handle it, but it sure would suck to get it back together just to find out it doesn't work. That was my biggest fear. Why not get a manual and read through it to see how much work it is.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
